1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,790
April twelfth, nineteen eighty-two.

2
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:06,770
Four in the morning, at Vostok Station,
in the middle of Antarctica.

3
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:09,750
Outside, it is far below minus fifty-five.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,890
In eleven days, the sun will
set for four months.

5
00:00:14,130 --> 00:00:16,300
A power technician wakes up to smoke.

6
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:18,260
The power station is on fire.

7
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:21,000
In about fifteen minutes, the whole
building is burning.

8
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:23,280
The fire extinguishers barely work.

9
00:00:24,404 --> 00:00:26,614
By morning, the station has
no electricity,

10
00:00:26,924 --> 00:00:29,874
no heating, and no power to
melt snow for water.

11
00:00:30,184 --> 00:00:31,274
One man is dead.

12
00:00:31,544 --> 00:00:32,694
Twenty are left alive.

13
00:00:33,846 --> 00:00:35,306
And nobody can come for them.

14
00:00:35,346 --> 00:00:36,516
Not for seven months.

15
00:00:37,304 --> 00:00:41,814
Vostok is where the coldest air temperature
ever measured on Earth was recorded:

16
00:00:42,064 --> 00:00:44,194
minus eighty-nine point two Celsius.

17
00:00:44,744 --> 00:00:47,114
That's minus a hundred and
twenty-eight Fahrenheit.

18
00:00:47,584 --> 00:00:50,654
People have wintered there almost every
year since nineteen fifty-seven.

19
00:00:51,104 --> 00:00:54,114
So how do people survive at the
coldest place ever measured?

20
00:00:54,761 --> 00:00:58,291
The obvious answer is technology:
power plants, heating.

21
00:00:58,541 --> 00:01:01,621
But in nineteen eighty-two, those
twenty men lost all of it,

22
00:01:01,661 --> 00:01:03,391
and they still got through the winter.

23
00:01:04,594 --> 00:01:06,044
The second answer: rescue.

24
00:01:06,314 --> 00:01:08,824
If something goes wrong, you
fly people out.

25
00:01:09,094 --> 00:01:11,644
But at Vostok, for about nine
months a year,

26
00:01:11,734 --> 00:01:14,484
no plane or tractor is
supposed to come at all.

27
00:01:15,591 --> 00:01:16,681
So it's something else.

28
00:01:16,951 --> 00:01:17,941
First, why here.

29
00:01:18,211 --> 00:01:20,921
Then, what this place does to a
body and to a machine.

30
00:01:21,211 --> 00:01:23,161
Then we'll go back to the
night of the fire.

31
00:01:23,391 --> 00:01:26,131
And at the end, we'll see the lesson
Vostok learned from it,

32
00:01:26,171 --> 00:01:28,401
which is still shaping the
station right now.

33
00:01:32,945 --> 00:01:37,915
In the nineteen fifties, the world's scientists
planned the International Geophysical Year.

34
00:01:38,465 --> 00:01:39,995
America took the South Pole.

35
00:01:40,185 --> 00:01:43,555
So the Soviet Union took the two
other poles nobody wanted:

36
00:01:43,705 --> 00:01:47,355
the South Geomagnetic Pole, and the
Pole of Inaccessibility.

37
00:01:48,532 --> 00:01:50,382
Vostok was built on the first.

38
00:01:50,832 --> 00:01:52,962
On December sixteenth,
nineteen fifty-seven,

39
00:01:53,352 --> 00:01:56,492
a tractor train arrived and set up
camp, on flat snow,

40
00:01:56,532 --> 00:01:59,082
three and a half kilometres
above sea level.

41
00:01:59,252 --> 00:02:01,442
That's about eleven thousand
five hundred feet.

42
00:02:02,592 --> 00:02:06,602
The nearest coast, at Mirny, was about
fourteen hundred kilometres away.

43
00:02:06,952 --> 00:02:09,582
That's London to Rome, or
Chicago to Denver.

44
00:02:09,832 --> 00:02:10,882
And there was no road.

45
00:02:11,212 --> 00:02:12,422
Only tracks in the snow.

46
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:16,800
The first winter, the thermometer
fell to minus eighty-seven.

47
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:20,760
In nineteen sixty, it reached minus
eighty-eight point three,

48
00:02:20,990 --> 00:02:21,780
a world record.

49
00:02:22,859 --> 00:02:25,659
That winter, the station
chief, Vasily Sidorov,

50
00:02:25,699 --> 00:02:27,709
told reporters what the cold did.

51
00:02:28,199 --> 00:02:31,909
Diesel fuel turned into a sticky mass.
Antifreeze crystallised.

52
00:02:32,139 --> 00:02:33,189
Metal became brittle.

53
00:02:34,393 --> 00:02:37,263
Then the frame of the main diesel
generator cracked.

54
00:02:37,493 --> 00:02:38,543
They could not weld it.

55
00:02:38,633 --> 00:02:41,683
At that height, there was not enough
oxygen for the welding torch.

56
00:02:42,843 --> 00:02:44,213
That was the first warning.

57
00:02:44,443 --> 00:02:47,033
At Vostok, the diesel is not
just one machine.

58
00:02:47,363 --> 00:02:49,233
It's light, heat and water at once.

59
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:54,410
Even before the cold, there is the air.

60
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:56,990
The station sits at three and a
half thousand metres,

61
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:01,210
but the polar air is so thin that your
lungs think it's four thousand.

62
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:04,450
There is nearly forty percent less
oxygen in every breath.

63
00:03:05,672 --> 00:03:09,042
New arrivals get headaches,
dizziness, and trouble sleeping.

64
00:03:09,432 --> 00:03:14,392
A station doctor, Sergey Bushmanov, says
people often lose five to ten kilos,

65
00:03:14,432 --> 00:03:16,522
ten to twenty pounds, in
their first month.

66
00:03:17,634 --> 00:03:18,484
Then the rules.

67
00:03:18,654 --> 00:03:22,844
At minus seventy and below, you may not stay
outside for more than ten minutes.

68
00:03:23,214 --> 00:03:27,504
A mask is compulsory, because one deep
breath can frostbite your airways.

69
00:03:27,694 --> 00:03:31,044
And at least half of every new crew must
have wintered at Vostok before.

70
00:03:32,291 --> 00:03:33,991
There is no liquid water here.

71
00:03:34,031 --> 00:03:35,741
Every drop starts as snow.

72
00:03:36,031 --> 00:03:39,161
The crew cuts blocks from a snow
quarry, carries them inside,

73
00:03:39,291 --> 00:03:41,181
and melts them, with heat from the diesel.

74
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:46,189
And from late April to late August, the
sun does not rise at all.

75
00:03:46,359 --> 00:03:48,289
That is when the real cold comes.

76
00:03:48,479 --> 00:03:51,449
In August, the average temperature
is minus sixty-eight.

77
00:03:52,793 --> 00:03:54,363
That's why the planes stop.

78
00:03:54,753 --> 00:03:59,263
From late February to mid-November, the air at
Vostok stays below minus fifty-five.

79
00:03:59,633 --> 00:04:02,483
Too cold for any plane or
tractor to come in.

80
00:04:03,543 --> 00:04:08,093
So every summer, everything the station needs
for winter has to arrive by road,

81
00:04:08,563 --> 00:04:11,333
fourteen hundred kilometres by
tractor across the ice.

82
00:04:11,903 --> 00:04:14,873
Today the trains leave from Progress
Station on the coast.

83
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:18,670
The tractors run on the same
fuel they carry.

84
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:21,130
About a third of it is burned
on the way there.

85
00:04:21,721 --> 00:04:23,711
And when the trains fail to bring enough,

86
00:04:23,921 --> 00:04:25,211
Vostok simply closes.

87
00:04:25,621 --> 00:04:28,081
It has been shut for the winter
at least three times,

88
00:04:28,121 --> 00:04:30,011
because the fuel did not arrive.

89
00:04:31,166 --> 00:04:34,176
So the whole station lives on
one thing: diesel,

90
00:04:34,246 --> 00:04:37,016
turned into heat and light by
one power station.

91
00:04:37,406 --> 00:04:40,596
And in nineteen eighty-two, that
power station burned down.

92
00:04:43,696 --> 00:04:49,006
In January nineteen eighty-two, the twenty-seventh Soviet
expedition arrived at Vostok. Twenty-one men.

93
00:04:49,156 --> 00:04:50,616
Their leader was Pyotr Astakhov.

94
00:04:50,656 --> 00:04:52,966
He had led Vostok before, and
Americans knew him.

95
00:04:53,256 --> 00:04:55,386
In nineteen sixty-seven,
according to his biography,

96
00:04:55,536 --> 00:04:58,256
he climbed twenty-seven metres up a
radio mast at the South Pole,

97
00:04:58,296 --> 00:05:00,246
at minus seventy, to fix an antenna.

98
00:05:00,556 --> 00:05:02,926
There's an Astakhov Glacier in
Antarctica today.

99
00:05:03,464 --> 00:05:05,314
In March, one of them fell ill.

100
00:05:05,704 --> 00:05:08,934
A plane flew in at below minus
sixty to take him out.

101
00:05:09,244 --> 00:05:13,054
The doctor's notes call it the only flight
like that in Vostok's history.

102
00:05:13,324 --> 00:05:15,654
Then the last plane left, and
the winter began.

103
00:05:16,849 --> 00:05:20,449
On April twelfth, a power
technician, Sergei Kuznetsov,

104
00:05:20,489 --> 00:05:21,579
woke up to smoke.

105
00:05:21,949 --> 00:05:26,379
The fire probably started in the wiring of a
small annex next to the machine hall,

106
00:05:26,449 --> 00:05:27,679
where the mechanics slept.

107
00:05:28,865 --> 00:05:31,375
The head of the power station,
Alexei Karpenko,

108
00:05:31,505 --> 00:05:33,195
went in to shut down the diesels.

109
00:05:33,465 --> 00:05:34,655
He died in the smoke.

110
00:05:35,606 --> 00:05:39,316
The others threw snow and fired
extinguishers. Nothing worked.

111
00:05:39,606 --> 00:05:41,016
The aluminium building melted.

112
00:05:41,226 --> 00:05:42,656
All the generators were lost.

113
00:05:43,650 --> 00:05:45,120
But one thing went their way.

114
00:05:45,270 --> 00:05:48,340
The wind changed, and the flames
missed the fuel store,

115
00:05:48,470 --> 00:05:50,220
dozens of barrels of diesel.

116
00:05:50,610 --> 00:05:53,220
The doctor, Arkady Maksimov, wrote later:

117
00:05:53,350 --> 00:05:55,820
without fuel, we would have had no
chance of surviving.

118
00:05:57,109 --> 00:05:59,889
Now the station was cold and dark,
in the polar night,

119
00:05:59,929 --> 00:06:01,599
and nobody in the world knew.

120
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:07,030
A driller, Boris Moiseev, got a small
generator from the drilling rig working.

121
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:08,850
It was just enough for the radio.

122
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:13,270
On the evening of April thirteenth, about
sixteen hours after the fire,

123
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:15,890
Vostok finally reached the outside world.

124
00:06:17,050 --> 00:06:18,860
Evacuation was impossible.

125
00:06:19,190 --> 00:06:21,900
No plane could land, and no
tractor could get through.

126
00:06:21,950 --> 00:06:23,930
So the men made a decision:
they would stay,

127
00:06:23,970 --> 00:06:25,080
and finish the winter.

128
00:06:26,310 --> 00:06:29,600
They found an old kerosene stove and
heated the mess room.

129
00:06:29,910 --> 00:06:32,440
Then they made five stoves out
of gas cylinders,

130
00:06:32,570 --> 00:06:34,260
burning diesel drip by drip.

131
00:06:35,351 --> 00:06:38,301
Next to a stove, it was plus
twenty-five at head height.

132
00:06:38,551 --> 00:06:40,981
Two metres away, by the wall, it was zero.

133
00:06:41,271 --> 00:06:43,321
They took turns at night to
watch the fire.

134
00:06:44,417 --> 00:06:48,027
They made candles from paraffin, with
wicks of asbestos cord.

135
00:06:48,257 --> 00:06:50,707
They melted snow for water on the stoves.

136
00:06:51,057 --> 00:06:54,947
The cook baked bread by sticking the dough
to the inside walls of a stove.

137
00:06:56,163 --> 00:06:57,353
And the work went on.

138
00:06:57,503 --> 00:07:01,373
The meteorologist, Vello Park, kept up
the full weather programme,

139
00:07:01,523 --> 00:07:06,153
every observation, even after his bosses
cancelled all science at Vostok.

140
00:07:07,290 --> 00:07:09,400
Outside, the winter fell to
minus eighty-five.

141
00:07:10,243 --> 00:07:14,113
Later, Moiseev revived an older
generator from cold storage,

142
00:07:14,203 --> 00:07:15,913
so they had more power for light.

143
00:07:16,789 --> 00:07:19,689
In early November, the first
plane of spring flew in,

144
00:07:19,729 --> 00:07:22,679
with a new generator and four men
from the next crew.

145
00:07:23,109 --> 00:07:27,859
About two weeks later, a tractor train arrived
with materials for a new power station.

146
00:07:29,084 --> 00:07:32,674
From the fire to the tractor train: two
hundred and twenty-seven days.

147
00:07:33,523 --> 00:07:35,313
Every man got a state award.

148
00:07:35,743 --> 00:07:39,013
Astakhov was nominated for Hero
of the Soviet Union,

149
00:07:39,063 --> 00:07:42,673
but, by his biography's account,
powerful people blocked it.

150
00:07:45,715 --> 00:07:48,525
One year later, Vostok broke a
record that still stands.

151
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:53,600
In July nineteen eighty-three, the next crew
watched the temperature fall about two

152
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:55,890
and a half degrees every day, for a week.

153
00:07:56,913 --> 00:08:00,223
On July twenty-first, at five
forty-five in the morning,

154
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:04,653
Moscow time, the meteorologists on
duty, Karpyuk and Lazarev,

155
00:08:04,693 --> 00:08:07,743
read the thermometer: minus
eighty-nine point two.

156
00:08:08,033 --> 00:08:10,063
Minus a hundred and
twenty-eight Fahrenheit.

157
00:08:11,324 --> 00:08:14,434
That's seventy degrees Celsius
colder than your freezer.

158
00:08:14,684 --> 00:08:18,854
And more than twenty degrees colder than the
coldest towns where people live,

159
00:08:18,944 --> 00:08:20,714
like Oymyakon, in Siberia.

160
00:08:22,125 --> 00:08:24,175
Scientists later found out why.

161
00:08:24,445 --> 00:08:27,575
For a whole week, there was no
cloud and almost no wind,

162
00:08:27,685 --> 00:08:30,485
and a pool of the coldest air sat
right over the station,

163
00:08:30,525 --> 00:08:32,375
with no warm air getting in.

164
00:08:33,516 --> 00:08:38,136
Satellites have since seen colder spots:
small hollows on the high ice ridge,

165
00:08:38,176 --> 00:08:41,206
where the snow surface reached
about minus ninety-eight.

166
00:08:41,536 --> 00:08:43,526
But that is the snow, not the air.

167
00:08:43,716 --> 00:08:46,446
The coldest air ever measured
is still Vostok's.

168
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:51,000
In a normal year, Vostok averages
about minus fifty-five.

169
00:08:51,470 --> 00:08:54,060
By some estimates, that's about
the average on Mars.

170
00:08:55,113 --> 00:08:58,203
But the strangest thing about
Vostok is not in the air.

171
00:08:58,373 --> 00:08:59,263
It's under the floor.

172
00:09:02,078 --> 00:09:04,988
Under nearly four kilometres of
ice, there is a lake.

173
00:09:05,338 --> 00:09:08,368
Lake Vostok is two hundred and
forty kilometres long,

174
00:09:08,498 --> 00:09:13,008
nearly the size of Lake Ontario, kept liquid
by the weight of the ice above it.

175
00:09:13,218 --> 00:09:16,128
Reaching it is why scientists put
up with all this cold.

176
00:09:16,815 --> 00:09:19,665
For decades, the crew drilled
down, collecting ice.

177
00:09:20,095 --> 00:09:22,235
Russia, France and America
drilled together,

178
00:09:22,275 --> 00:09:24,045
and split the ice three ways.

179
00:09:24,355 --> 00:09:27,605
The cores held air from more than four
hundred thousand years ago.

180
00:09:27,735 --> 00:09:31,235
They showed that carbon dioxide and
temperature rose and fell together,

181
00:09:31,275 --> 00:09:32,765
through four ice ages.

182
00:09:33,867 --> 00:09:36,977
In twenty twelve, the drill finally
reached the lake water.

183
00:09:37,147 --> 00:09:38,137
Did they find life?

184
00:09:38,547 --> 00:09:41,257
Reports of a new bacterium turned
out to be contamination.

185
00:09:41,827 --> 00:09:45,097
A Russian geneticist, Vladimir
Korolyov, put it bluntly:

186
00:09:45,367 --> 00:09:46,537
all of them were contaminants.

187
00:09:46,867 --> 00:09:49,297
So far, nothing alive has been confirmed.

188
00:09:51,976 --> 00:09:55,086
So how do people survive at the
coldest place ever measured?

189
00:09:55,875 --> 00:09:57,205
Not with buildings alone.

190
00:09:57,455 --> 00:09:59,875
In nineteen eighty-two, the
power station burned,

191
00:09:59,915 --> 00:10:01,065
and the men survived.

192
00:10:01,155 --> 00:10:02,185
And not with rescue.

193
00:10:02,415 --> 00:10:03,025
There was none.

194
00:10:04,038 --> 00:10:06,328
Vostok survives on three things.

195
00:10:06,738 --> 00:10:09,568
Fuel, dragged fourteen hundred
kilometres by tractor.

196
00:10:09,758 --> 00:10:11,508
A crew where half have done it before.

197
00:10:11,638 --> 00:10:12,948
And a spare for everything.

198
00:10:14,121 --> 00:10:16,601
In nineteen eighty-two,
Vostok had the fuel,

199
00:10:16,641 --> 00:10:17,791
and it had the people.

200
00:10:18,081 --> 00:10:19,451
What it didn't have was a spare.

201
00:10:19,961 --> 00:10:22,751
Light, heat and water all lived
in one building.

202
00:10:22,921 --> 00:10:25,551
The only spares were the ones they
dug out of the snow.

203
00:10:26,762 --> 00:10:28,912
The new Vostok was built on that lesson.

204
00:10:29,362 --> 00:10:32,912
In January twenty twenty-four, a new
winter complex started work:

205
00:10:33,102 --> 00:10:36,192
five blue modules, a hundred and
forty metres long,

206
00:10:36,342 --> 00:10:39,302
longer than a football field,
standing on thirty-six legs,

207
00:10:39,342 --> 00:10:40,972
so the snow blows underneath.

208
00:10:41,633 --> 00:10:43,863
Its walls are eighty centimetres thick.

209
00:10:44,033 --> 00:10:46,463
It has a sauna, a gym and a cinema.

210
00:10:46,813 --> 00:10:48,503
It has four main generators.

211
00:10:48,673 --> 00:10:51,093
And two backup generators, in a
separate module,

212
00:10:51,133 --> 00:10:52,903
far from the main power plant.

213
00:10:54,072 --> 00:10:56,342
Getting it there took more
than three years.

214
00:10:56,552 --> 00:10:59,982
The first ship broke a propeller off
Angola and had to turn back.

215
00:11:00,332 --> 00:11:02,712
The modules finally crossed
the ice by tractor,

216
00:11:02,752 --> 00:11:04,362
the same way as the fuel.

217
00:11:05,571 --> 00:11:08,481
And at the end of every summer, the
last plane still leaves,

218
00:11:08,591 --> 00:11:10,381
and the station is on its own again.

219
00:11:11,243 --> 00:11:15,933
Would you spend one winter at Vostok, knowing
that no plane can come for nine months?

220
00:11:16,063 --> 00:11:17,093
Tell me in the comments.

221
00:11:17,423 --> 00:11:19,793
And tell me which place we
should explain next.

222
00:11:20,924 --> 00:11:24,084
Alexei Karpenko is buried on a
small island near Mirny,

223
00:11:24,124 --> 00:11:25,054
on the coast.

224
00:11:25,584 --> 00:11:28,994
And every April, when the last plane has
gone and the sun goes down,

225
00:11:29,044 --> 00:11:31,774
the Vostok crew knows exactly
what keeps them alive.

226
00:11:32,064 --> 00:11:32,794
It's a fire.

227
00:11:33,084 --> 00:11:34,804
The right kind of fire, in
the right place,

228
00:11:34,844 --> 00:11:35,654
with a spare.

229
00:11:35,944 --> 00:11:37,094
This was How It's Run.
